  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1025 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nWednesday through Sunday:\n\nOn Wednesday, high pressure slides southeast into Manitoba and a \ndeepening trough in the jet dives southeast from the Dakotas. Low\npressure develops over IA / IL in response to the trough \nWednesday morning, with the trough and low tracking east into the\nOhio River Valley / eastern Great Lakes Wednesday night. QG ascent\nfrom the approaching trough and developing low create 35-50% \nrain chances across the CWA Wednesday, with a developing north \nbreeze and highs in the low 50s. \n\nAs both pressure systems intensify and the low exits east, north\nwinds become gusty across Lake Michigan Wednesday night through\nFriday, with Thursday featuring the strongest winds. Some 30-40 \nmph wind gusts are looking likely across far eastern WI Thursday,\nparticularly along the Lake MI shoreline. \n\nHealthy lake effect convection tends to emerge with Lake Water\nTemp to 850 mb temperature deltas over 13 Celsius; cold air \narriving late this week could max out the temperature delta \ncloser to 17 to 23 Celsius. With steady north winds down the long\naxis of the lake from Wednesday night through Saturday, we expect\na north-south oriented single band of lake effect convection on\nLake MI, which could wobble east-west at times, occasionally \nmaking landfall on either side of the lake depending on the exact\nwind direction. For counties bordering the lake in our CWA, this\nwill mean ~20-50% chances for precipitation at times from\nWednesday night through Friday, with chances generally < 10% into\nSaturday as winds turn northwest. Rain is favored with the vast \nmajority of these showers, though there will be periods of time\n(mainly in the overnight and early morning hours) where some\nsnowflakes could mix in. I'll detail 2 specific timeframes below.\n\nLate Wednesday night into early Thursday morning (inland temps in\nthe low/mid 30s, shoreline temps around 40) we could see a few \nsnowflakes mix in with the rain if the precip were to travel far \nenough inland (west edge of shoreline counties, or the 2nd tier of\ncounties). And from Thursday evening into Friday morning (inland \ntemps briefly reach the upper 20s, shoreline temps bottom out in \nthe mid 30s) we could see some rain-snow mix or even pure \nsnowfall at times. There's no guarantee (yet) that the lake effect\nprecip will make landfall during these specific timeframes, and \noutside of that it'll likely be all rain. Additionally, the \nfigures I've provided for temperatures are the 2-meter air temps. \nGetting the land-surface or pavement temps to fall below freezing\ncould prove difficult with the accompanying lake-effect cloud\ncover, and as such we are not currently forecasting \naccumulating snow. That said, trends in the temperature forecast\nwill be followed closely; things could change.\n\nWinds turn northwest and decelerate into Saturday, pushing any\nlingering lake effect precip away from WI. Dry weather expected\nthrough the weekend, with highs rising from the 40s (Saturday) \ninto the 50s (Sunday). \n\nSheppard",
